But if you're going to have backtracking in a game like this, the barriers should have been

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:16.260
closer together.

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:17.880
They shouldn't have been so open.

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:24.260
See, maybe I didn't have like, I just didn't get to get into a situation where it, it hurt

00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:28.200
me that much. But I always felt like when I had to backtrack it, it never ended up being too far.

00:42:28.220 --> 00:42:29.740
I was like, I was pleased. I was like, oh, okay.

00:42:29.800 --> 00:42:31.000
At least it's not that big of a space.

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:34.300
It ends up being like three to four minutes of backtracking, I think sometimes.

00:42:34.600 --> 00:42:37.600
But like, that doesn't sound like a lot, but it is a lot.

00:42:37.880 --> 00:42:38.800
When you're playing. It's a lot.

00:42:38.960 --> 00:42:39.900
You're playing. Yeah.

00:42:40.020 --> 00:42:43.420
And then you have to like also, you know, you have to be on your guard when it comes to

00:42:43.420 --> 00:42:47.400
enemies because your energy is mostly going down, not really counting up.

00:42:47.660 --> 00:42:48.780
For me, that was the biggest thing.

00:42:48.940 --> 00:42:54.680
Like if you get to the end of a really hard corridor and you barely escape, you know, and

00:42:54.680 --> 00:42:57.940
then it's a dead end and you've got to turn back around and go back through it.

00:42:58.100 --> 00:43:00.040
Right. It's just about like the biggest sigh.

00:43:00.160 --> 00:43:01.920
Yeah. And you're like, all right, I guess I'm going to do this.

00:43:02.080 --> 00:43:06.120
Or if you're like me and you don't realize that the missile doors take five missiles,

00:43:06.220 --> 00:43:07.700
because what is that?

00:43:07.700 --> 00:43:10.320
You shoot it three times with a missile and nothing happens.

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:11.720
So you don't want to waste the rest of your missiles.

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:14.620
Yeah. So I figured, oh, okay, this is, this must be for something else.

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:15.880
That is an odd thing.

00:43:16.080 --> 00:43:20.620
Yeah. And so that happened to me where I would go through, I went through a really hard corridor,

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:25.680
got through a missile door that I was there specifically to get, because I remembered it

00:43:25.680 --> 00:43:29.260
was in a certain spot, shot it with the missile and it didn't open.

00:43:29.820 --> 00:43:33.440
And I said, all right, I guess I have to find a different kind of door.

00:43:33.440 --> 00:43:35.440
Yeah. And it went back through the same corridor.

00:43:35.720 --> 00:43:37.480
I've been playing. Had to go back through again.

00:43:37.660 --> 00:43:40.060
It was just. Yeah. I've been playing Metroid for many years.

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:42.680
The other, you know, like all the games, including this one.

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:45.240
And I didn't just know about the five missile thing.

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:49.260
Like, it's just a rule, but I'm curious, like how, you know, like Sam has explained it.

00:43:49.480 --> 00:43:52.160
He thought it was just one and you'd be done and assumed he did wrong.

00:43:52.340 --> 00:43:54.600
You guys clearly found out eventually.

00:43:54.760 --> 00:43:56.460
How did you find out that it was five?

00:43:56.660 --> 00:44:02.560
Well, I found out it was five just because I knew that, like, I knew from osmosis, like,

00:44:02.660 --> 00:44:05.860
because I never played these games before, but I knew that the missiles open these doors.

00:44:05.860 --> 00:44:09.560
So I'm like, okay, some number has to open this.

00:44:09.560 --> 00:44:14.400
But if I was a kid that just got this game and had didn't have friends that had already

00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:18.480
played it and told me this or didn't look it up, like I would think the same thing.

00:44:18.600 --> 00:44:19.940
I would move on to the next thing.

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:25.340
Yeah. So I had, you know, I ran into the door first, couldn't, couldn't, couldn't get through it.

00:44:25.380 --> 00:44:27.640
So I was kind of thinking, oh, there must be something I have to get.

00:44:27.780 --> 00:44:32.000
And I went out searching and I found the missiles next, came back to the door and shot it.

00:44:32.080 --> 00:44:34.880
And I kind of just had like a no, like this does have to be with it.

00:44:35.000 --> 00:44:38.160
And I just kind of like, I was like, just kept shooting out of like desperation.

00:44:38.760 --> 00:44:43.060
Well, that must be how people find out though, too, if I think about it, you know, it's like

00:44:43.060 --> 00:44:47.080
eventually you're going to realize like, you know, you might go back and try and find like

00:44:47.080 --> 00:44:48.120
the morph ball bomb.

00:44:48.460 --> 00:44:50.940
And then when that doesn't work, you're finally just going to be like, you know what?

00:44:50.940 --> 00:44:55.500
I'm just going to shoot this thing with missiles, you know, like with everything I've got until

00:44:55.500 --> 00:44:59.440
something works. And I think that winds up being a part of the game.

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:04.500
It's like there just comes a time where like you hit a room and you're like, have no idea

00:45:04.500 --> 00:45:07.360
what to do. You just shoot everything.

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:11.440
You morph ball hoping that like a passage is open somewhere.

00:45:11.660 --> 00:45:15.360
You bomb the floors hoping that one of them has like a little thing.

00:45:15.620 --> 00:45:20.600
And sometimes they have like an energy tank or something, you know, so many PS1 games where

00:45:20.600 --> 00:45:21.780
that's just how I played the game.

00:45:22.020 --> 00:45:28.700
It's so strange. And then but then like once you find the thing like that's been blocking it, you know, like

00:45:28.700 --> 00:45:31.920
the whatever the puzzle was, it just becomes so obvious.

00:45:31.920 --> 00:45:33.180
And then you get mad at yourself.

00:45:33.380 --> 00:45:36.980
I don't know if it was obvious because no, there are parts that are not obvious.

00:45:37.200 --> 00:45:40.240
Like my reaction was more like, are you kidding me?

00:45:40.340 --> 00:45:44.300
Like I was supposed to know that or I know that they don't expect you to know that, but

00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:46.000
it should be more obvious.

00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:50.700
I do think that the five missile thing, like I think I lucked into finding that out, but I

00:45:50.700 --> 00:45:52.460
think the puzzle was getting the missiles.

00:45:52.960 --> 00:45:57.140
So to make it so then you have to use five of them when it's not really like explained,

00:45:57.280 --> 00:46:01.420
it is a little is a little like I could see why they'll confuse people and not feel like

00:46:01.420 --> 00:46:03.900
the best designed mechanic.

00:46:04.060 --> 00:46:04.880
Yeah. Let's be honest.

00:46:05.180 --> 00:46:11.700
In 1987, this game sucks if you don't have friends, you know, like this is a game where

00:46:11.700 --> 00:46:15.060
I don't care if you're a kid or an adult, you better have other people to talk to about

00:46:15.060 --> 00:46:18.020
this game because you're going to figure things out that they haven't and they're going to

00:46:18.020 --> 00:46:19.140
figure out things that you haven't.

00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:24.580
And this is one of those communication based games where, you know, you don't have the

00:46:24.580 --> 00:46:25.940
internet. The meta game.

00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:31.900
Yeah, exactly. You need to kind of like get together and think of those people as your galactic federation

00:46:31.900 --> 00:46:36.860
phoning into your Samus to tell her the information on how to get through the next door or something.

00:46:37.540 --> 00:46:43.420
But yeah, if you don't have those resources available to you, I don't think that getting

00:46:43.420 --> 00:46:47.720
stuck in these kind of games and going to the internet is cheating.

00:46:48.040 --> 00:46:48.840
No. Okay.

00:46:49.040 --> 00:46:52.840
This is different than the Solomon's key dilemma that we talked about in that episode where

00:46:52.840 --> 00:46:55.980
like when you're actually solving a puzzle.

00:46:56.340 --> 00:47:00.700
Yeah. That's when things get cheating when you go to online because it's not satisfying.

00:47:00.700 --> 00:47:02.000
This is a literal puzzle.

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:06.420
Yeah. Right. I think there's kind of like levels of like how you want to face it.

00:47:06.420 --> 00:47:08.920
I guess I wouldn't say it's like, yeah, you're cheating.

00:47:09.100 --> 00:47:13.160
It's just like, do you want to beat it exactly the way that it was intended where you have

00:47:13.160 --> 00:47:13.960
to figure it all out yourself?

00:47:14.180 --> 00:47:16.800
It's going to be a real challenge and it might not be fun.

00:47:17.080 --> 00:47:20.960
So it's like, or do you want to like, yeah, you get some help on, you know, it's fine either

00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:28.500
way. I think the, the attitude has evolved over time because maybe in 86, 87, that would be

00:47:28.500 --> 00:47:30.280
like, oh, you spoiled it for me.

00:47:30.340 --> 00:47:33.880
Or like, oh, you, you want me to tell you the, the, the answer you loser.

00:47:34.140 --> 00:47:38.720
And I think now it's actually become like, it is just a part of games now.

00:47:38.840 --> 00:47:43.460
Like if you look at Dark Souls, there'll be writing on the floor that other players have

00:47:43.460 --> 00:47:52.980
left for you. Or, um, let's go back to PT where, um, the, the actual, like obscure puzzle that they put

00:47:52.980 --> 00:47:57.220
into that teaser could not be solved unless the internet got involved.

00:47:57.340 --> 00:48:01.000
Like it, it's a, it's a whole new attitude.

00:48:01.300 --> 00:48:05.060
Yeah. And you put in like Metal Gear Solid five where they're like, there's still a community

00:48:05.060 --> 00:48:09.180
of people trying to disarm nukes for no apparent reason whatsoever.

00:48:09.180 --> 00:48:11.220
And it's like, that's just really cool.

00:48:11.220 --> 00:48:15.420
But I think your Dark Souls example is the best one because when, when I first played

00:48:15.420 --> 00:48:19.320
that game and I'm not good at those games, I barely got far at all, but I thought like,

00:48:19.600 --> 00:48:25.520
wow, what a neat feature that like, there's just randomly chosen community points that

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:26.860
will like warn you about things.

00:48:26.860 --> 00:48:30.120
Like watch out for the guy with the bow and arrow on the top of the castle.

00:48:30.180 --> 00:48:31.820
It can also troll you really hard though.

00:48:31.840 --> 00:48:32.780
Yeah. That's true too.

00:48:32.780 --> 00:48:35.360
But like, I just think like, wow, it's really interesting idea.

00:48:35.360 --> 00:48:40.780
And like, imagine if that had like, you know, a Metroid feature on Nintendo switch online,

00:48:40.780 --> 00:48:41.880
which won't happen. No.

00:48:42.160 --> 00:48:46.840
But imagine if it was like on the Nintendo switch online, you can enable a mode where

00:48:46.840 --> 00:48:50.720
the community can kind of give you tips and be like, Hey, check around this area, you

00:48:50.720 --> 00:48:55.160
know, for bomb, for bombing things, you know, or like use your suit, use your missile, but

00:48:55.160 --> 00:48:57.440
doesn't tell you where, like, I think that would have been a cool thing.

00:48:57.860 --> 00:49:01.520
Yeah. Even, even kind of Mario maker sort of does with the, like telling you where other people

00:49:01.520 --> 00:49:02.440
died. Yeah.

00:49:02.460 --> 00:49:03.700
It's not like a community of people.

00:49:03.820 --> 00:49:07.560
It's not, those people aren't telling you, but you're seeing like, Oh, 800 people died in

00:49:07.560 --> 00:49:10.880
that spot. Like something going to happen when I go to another, there's another thing that dark

00:49:10.880 --> 00:49:11.780
souls did really well.

00:49:11.800 --> 00:49:13.300
Mario maker did have hints too, as well though.

00:49:13.500 --> 00:49:18.880
Like, that would, that would tell you like, you know, make sure you do this or, or troll

00:49:18.880 --> 00:49:20.240
you. Yes. There will always be trolls.

00:49:20.420 --> 00:49:26.040
And in fact, I'm quite confident that the equivalent of trolling back then in like 1987

00:49:26.040 --> 00:49:28.800
was like just giving a bogus password.

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:30.000
Yeah. You know what I mean?

00:49:30.320 --> 00:49:33.780
Oh, this password makes it so this way you are win the game.

00:49:33.940 --> 00:49:35.280
Like it just doesn't happen.

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:37.160
Yeah. Or even just like playground talk.

00:49:37.260 --> 00:49:41.960
Like, do you know, if you jump on this 600 times, like you get an extra gun and you're

00:49:41.960 --> 00:49:43.380
like trying to do it over and over again.

00:49:43.480 --> 00:49:47.580
Or I remember, uh, uh, we'll, I'll do this and we can stop talking about this.

00:49:47.760 --> 00:49:54.000
I know that, uh, one of the magazines, the gaming magazines would have an April fool's

00:49:54.000 --> 00:49:55.620
joke. Oh, game and farcer.

00:49:55.860 --> 00:49:57.100
That was the one I remember.

00:49:57.260 --> 00:50:00.460
Game informer would become on April on their April issue.

00:50:00.460 --> 00:50:02.280
They would become game and farcer.

00:50:02.440 --> 00:50:03.600
Were they? Yeah.

00:50:04.020 --> 00:50:05.860
And just for like, it was four pages.

00:50:06.160 --> 00:50:10.480
Okay. Uh, and it would have some of the funniest things that I've ever read.

00:50:10.480 --> 00:50:13.100
There were some more devious ones though that actually looked like tips.

00:50:13.380 --> 00:50:21.720
Yeah. These ones were very clearly jokes, but, um, I do remember I got pranked so hard a year

00:50:21.720 --> 00:50:23.420
after the issue had come out.

00:50:23.420 --> 00:50:27.320
So I, I collected Nintendo power and I was just like reading them.

00:50:27.600 --> 00:50:29.540
I would bring them to school for our reading time.

00:50:29.700 --> 00:50:30.940
Cause my teachers would allow that.

00:50:31.080 --> 00:50:32.000
They'd be like, yeah, Nintendo power, whatever.

00:50:32.140 --> 00:50:33.080
It's a magazine read.

00:50:33.380 --> 00:50:34.380
And I would read it.

00:50:34.460 --> 00:50:37.440
And I didn't realize I was reading the April issue of last year.

00:50:37.660 --> 00:50:43.520
And they were talking about warp pipe technology coming to like, you know, like to Japan.

00:50:43.900 --> 00:50:45.620
Like that they were actually just going to do it.

00:50:45.680 --> 00:50:49.460
And I remember like showing everybody in my class and people being like, their minds were

00:50:49.460 --> 00:50:51.640
blown. Like we were all going to be like super Mario.

00:50:51.640 --> 00:50:54.180
And I was like, in my head, I'm like, yeah, why wouldn't this happen?

00:50:54.800 --> 00:50:55.660
It's in the game.

00:50:55.840 --> 00:50:57.460
We should be able to extract that.

00:50:57.660 --> 00:51:03.560
Oh my God. So that's enough about April fools and saw, but I think that was like, that was a great

00:51:03.920 --> 00:51:07.180
conversation though. Because that's a lot of what this game comes down to.

00:51:22.500 --> 00:51:23.300
Real fast.

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:28.840
The boss battles, because I'm not, I'm not so sold that they feel like boss battles.

00:51:29.020 --> 00:51:31.740
It just feels like giant enemy encounters.

00:51:32.040 --> 00:51:35.940
Like, you know, just a larger enemy that you must deal with now.

00:51:36.100 --> 00:51:39.600
And they take a lot more hits, but a lot of hits, take a lot of hits, but the missiles

00:51:39.600 --> 00:51:40.620
are there for a reason.

00:51:40.620 --> 00:51:46.140
I mean, if there's one reason why you should have 250 missiles, it's strictly for the bosses.

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:50.840
You know, it's like, I don't think you need 250 missiles throughout the regular part of

00:51:50.840 --> 00:51:54.300
this game. There's no door that takes 250 million missiles to open.

00:51:54.700 --> 00:51:55.780
There's a secret door.

00:51:55.920 --> 00:51:57.520
250 million missiles.

00:51:58.140 --> 00:51:59.160
Sean, you had a thought.

00:51:59.160 --> 00:52:06.160
I just think that we aren't at the point yet where good boss design has really flourished

00:52:06.160 --> 00:52:11.880
yet. I think that, like, I think Castlevania did it pretty well, at least like to an extent.

00:52:12.080 --> 00:52:14.380
I really enjoyed Castlevania's boss battles, actually.

00:52:14.580 --> 00:52:19.200
Like, those are the only ones that, like, Kung Fu did not bring boss battles to me.

00:52:19.600 --> 00:52:21.580
Super Mario Brothers definitely does not.

00:52:21.720 --> 00:52:25.900
But at least Kung Fu had, like, they were events.

00:52:25.900 --> 00:52:34.100
Like, they're not great bosses, but they, you had to prepare for their patterns and their

00:52:34.100 --> 00:52:38.440
techniques. Whereas in this, it's just like, okay, there's, just shoot a bunch of times.

00:52:38.640 --> 00:52:40.340
Shoot the big thing a bunch of times.

00:52:40.360 --> 00:52:41.800
They're here. It's like a thing.

00:52:41.900 --> 00:52:45.080
You know, it's like, it's like Ridley isn't, I don't know, he just didn't seem like aware

00:52:45.080 --> 00:52:45.960
of my presence.

00:52:46.760 --> 00:52:49.420
Like, he just seemed like he was moving around the room.

00:52:49.420 --> 00:52:51.720
He's just always doing that. Whether you're there or not, that's just what he's doing.

00:52:51.920 --> 00:52:55.420
I wouldn't be surprised to find that out, that he's just always doing that because I was

00:52:55.420 --> 00:52:58.940
more or less, like, it was a pain in the neck to target him a lot.

00:52:59.100 --> 00:53:02.880
Like, I was shooting missiles and it just wasn't connecting because he'd be moving around.

00:53:02.880 --> 00:53:05.700
And I was like, are you avoiding me or am I avoiding you?

00:53:05.900 --> 00:53:08.280
Like, who is afraid of who here?

00:53:08.720 --> 00:53:13.180
So, yeah, I think another place where this game falls short is in its boss design.

00:53:13.660 --> 00:53:18.000
But it doesn't really feel like it's building up to those boss designs anyway, right?

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:23.380
Like, I mean, yes, that's the statue room and you have to, like, have them to get through.

00:53:23.580 --> 00:53:25.060
But, like, I don't know.

00:53:25.140 --> 00:53:28.980
I just never really thought, like, when I got to Kraid, I was like, oh, wow, like, things

00:53:28.980 --> 00:53:32.780
are about to go down. Nah, I didn't even know he was coming before that room.

00:53:32.880 --> 00:53:34.060
Like, really, if we're being honest.

00:53:34.220 --> 00:53:37.800
I was used to Kraid from Melee.

00:53:38.040 --> 00:53:41.620
Oh, yeah. Where Kraid is just this gigantic thing.

00:53:41.800 --> 00:53:44.300
And then when you get there, it's like, where's the real Kraid?

00:53:44.300 --> 00:53:46.180
You're just a little taller than me.

00:53:46.300 --> 00:53:49.320
Like, you're 6'4". Yeah, they would eventually retcon that, of course.

00:53:49.620 --> 00:53:51.980
But, like, not really retcon it, I guess.

00:53:52.100 --> 00:53:56.400
Like, obviously the real Metroid fans will give me the scoop on what really happens because

00:53:56.400 --> 00:53:58.040
Metroid is part of the timeline.

00:53:58.360 --> 00:54:02.520
But I just mean, like, when we see Kraid next in Super Metroid, he's gigantic.

00:54:02.820 --> 00:54:06.340
So, explain that one, Metroid fans.

00:54:06.880 --> 00:54:08.380
Seriously, at me on Twitter.

00:54:09.000 --> 00:54:10.580
At him. Okay.

00:54:11.540 --> 00:54:13.520
Now I have, like, an army coming after me.

00:54:24.840 --> 00:54:30.540
I guess, let me ask an interesting question for my last question of the game.

00:54:31.420 --> 00:54:34.260
What is satisfying about this game?

00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:39.020
What is, like, or the most satisfying thing about this game for each of you?

00:54:39.420 --> 00:54:40.840
I got one. Sure.

00:54:40.980 --> 00:54:46.500
Sam. It's, you'd mentioned this before, I think, but I think getting an item, getting an upgrade

00:54:46.500 --> 00:54:51.740
is the most satisfying thing because it just unlocks everything in your head automatically.

00:54:51.960 --> 00:54:56.080
You think, oh, every time that there was that thing that I passed, now I can do that.

00:54:56.300 --> 00:55:01.560
You know? And it's that moment of realization without having to actually slog through all the annoying

00:55:01.560 --> 00:55:05.940
parts yet that I think is the best.

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:07.000
Hard agree, Sam.

00:55:07.700 --> 00:55:11.260
Just for the sound effect alone, I would be like, that's the most satisfying part.

00:55:12.500 --> 00:55:18.640
But it's definitely just satisfying in general to have that eureka moment of, like, you didn't

00:55:18.640 --> 00:55:20.480
even know, like, the ice beam, right?

00:55:20.520 --> 00:55:23.120
You didn't know you were even going to get that if you didn't really, like, search through

00:55:23.120 --> 00:55:24.040
the manual. Yeah.

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:27.280
If you just find the ice beam, all of a sudden you're like, oh yeah, and I can freeze those

00:55:27.280 --> 00:55:28.660
guys and use them as platforms.

00:55:28.660 --> 00:55:32.940
Like, you know, it's just one of those things that, like, you can do.

00:55:33.100 --> 00:55:37.000
Or the high jump moment where you're like, oh, that's why I can finally jump to that area.

00:55:37.240 --> 00:55:39.940
Yes, exactly. A whole new way of exploring that world, too.

00:55:40.260 --> 00:55:42.080
Yeah. I agree with that one.

00:55:42.260 --> 00:55:46.780
The only thing I would add to it, too, is the actual, like, exploration beforehand, like,

00:55:46.940 --> 00:55:52.340
finding the items. So it's like, finding your way to the items, I think, also is very satisfying.

00:55:52.340 --> 00:55:56.500
But to add on to what Sam said, too, is that the, I know we kind of mentioned it before,

00:55:56.500 --> 00:56:02.480
but, like, once you have a lot of these power-ups, like, you feel significantly more powerful

00:56:02.480 --> 00:56:05.300
than you started. You feel like you have upgrades.

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:07.880
Like, they don't feel like weak upgrades.

00:56:08.100 --> 00:56:11.880
Yeah, the game definitely gets progression right, if nothing else.

00:56:12.220 --> 00:56:18.540
Sean? I mean, I would agree with you guys, but just for the sake of changing it up a little, I

00:56:18.540 --> 00:56:21.640
will talk about something a little different.

00:56:21.640 --> 00:56:27.260
I'd say that one of the most satisfying feelings is more just, like, satisfying, like, scratching

00:56:27.260 --> 00:56:30.120
an itch. Like, oh, you're in a weird position.

00:56:30.120 --> 00:56:33.040
You can't quite get to that part of your body.

00:56:33.040 --> 00:56:37.360
So it itches. And then finally, you find some way to get to it.

00:56:37.540 --> 00:56:38.660
And it's when... Yeah, it itches.

00:56:38.900 --> 00:56:46.560
Yeah. And it's when you you're so low on life that you just get that godforsaken, godforsaken

00:56:46.560 --> 00:56:51.220
beeping noise, and it's just going and going and going, and you finally get enough energy

00:56:51.220 --> 00:56:52.280
to make it stop.

00:56:52.660 --> 00:56:54.180
Because that is...

00:56:54.180 --> 00:57:01.140
That's true. Because that is the worst sound effect that I've had to, like, listen to for over a minute.

00:57:01.420 --> 00:57:04.040
You think that that's the suit making that noise?

00:57:04.260 --> 00:57:06.380
Like, panic? I would hope it's the suit.

00:57:06.380 --> 00:57:09.240
Yeah, like, and not just, like, but I meant, like, it's more for us.

00:57:09.520 --> 00:57:11.040
Yeah. But, like, do you think Samus...

00:57:11.040 --> 00:57:11.860
I think it's a diegetic noise.

00:57:12.160 --> 00:57:13.600
I think it's the cyber suit.

00:57:13.740 --> 00:57:16.000
Yeah. It's also funny, though, that, like, all the enemies are hearing it.

00:57:16.060 --> 00:57:18.080
Yeah, that's what I was going to say. It's like, all the enemies are alerted.

00:57:18.220 --> 00:57:20.080
Do you know? I want that.

00:57:21.040 --> 00:57:21.980
So, yeah, they did...

00:57:21.980 --> 00:57:24.240
Actually, yeah, the enemies don't become any more aggressive.

00:57:24.240 --> 00:57:26.180
That would be an interesting feature to add.

00:57:26.260 --> 00:57:28.420
Like, become more aggressive when you're low on health.

00:57:28.560 --> 00:57:29.740
But you become stronger.

00:57:30.020 --> 00:57:31.840
That'd be cool. That's a normal RPG trope.

00:57:36.500 --> 00:57:38.600
Okay, let's talk about development.

00:57:39.060 --> 00:57:42.360
The section where I give you guys a bunch of facts and you react.

00:57:43.380 --> 00:57:45.520
Let's do it. This is not as usually works.

00:57:45.800 --> 00:57:49.920
It's no secret that usually we don't have a lot to talk about when it comes to development

00:57:49.920 --> 00:57:53.360
because I'm scraping the bottom of the barrel for things about Athena.

00:57:54.540 --> 00:57:57.780
But for Metroid, we have a good trove here of information.

00:57:58.060 --> 00:58:01.400
So it's no surprise here, Sakamoto was inspired by Alien.

00:58:02.260 --> 00:58:07.700
But, like, not really because the development of this game was, like, going on for 10 months

00:58:07.700 --> 00:58:08.920
before he joined the project.

00:58:09.280 --> 00:58:12.980
So he's like, yeah, I was inspired by Alien, the movie, you know.

00:58:13.160 --> 00:58:14.460
Oh, the movie. Yeah, the movie.

00:58:14.600 --> 00:58:15.860
I know. First, I'm like, Alien.

00:58:16.180 --> 00:58:17.000
Everybody knows it.

00:58:17.100 --> 00:58:18.940
Well, yeah. Okay, I was being sarcastic.

00:58:18.940 --> 00:58:24.400
Yeah, yeah, yeah. I feel like if I say Alien, you know, if I said he's inspired by Aliens, that's up for

00:58:24.400 --> 00:58:28.180
debate, right? It's like all of a sudden it's like, oh, by Tim Allen.

00:58:28.400 --> 00:58:33.620
Tim Allen. It's a buzz light year, which didn't happen until nine years later.

00:58:33.620 --> 00:58:35.760
That's a pretty good connection, though. Man, he was space.

00:58:36.220 --> 00:58:37.240
Really was ahead of his time.

00:58:37.940 --> 00:58:40.860
Wow, okay. And that's the development.

00:58:41.100 --> 00:58:42.800
Yeah. That's the development in a nutshell.

00:58:45.140 --> 00:58:52.600
No. Basically, the only thing that I think about with the alien influence is like a person

00:58:52.600 --> 00:58:54.540
alone with a bunch of aliens.

00:58:54.800 --> 00:58:56.960
Okay, no, no, no. There's a lot of visual elements as well.

00:58:57.100 --> 00:58:59.800
Okay, so you got the space jockeys, or whatever the hell they're called.

00:58:59.920 --> 00:59:01.220
The jockeys. Space pirates?

00:59:01.460 --> 00:59:03.280
No, no, no, no. Metroid.

00:59:03.960 --> 00:59:07.820
So every little altar that you get an upgrade at.

00:59:07.860 --> 00:59:09.560
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah. Oh, the Chozo statues.

00:59:09.800 --> 00:59:11.480
Yeah. You call those space jockeys?

00:59:11.620 --> 00:59:13.140
Well, there's something jockeys.

00:59:13.280 --> 00:59:15.460
I forgot. Oh, and those are the guys from Prometheus, right?

00:59:15.580 --> 00:59:18.420
Let me, uh... Yeah, but in Alien, you see one.

00:59:18.600 --> 00:59:22.360
Yeah, okay. And it looks very similar to the Chozo statue thing.

00:59:22.420 --> 00:59:31.420
Huh. And you also have just the idea of a bio-engineered weapon that could end all life in the universe

00:59:31.420 --> 00:59:34.740
if it so wanted to, and all that kind of good stuff.

00:59:34.880 --> 00:59:36.300
And a female main star.

00:59:36.480 --> 00:59:37.380
And Ripley.

00:59:37.980 --> 00:59:39.820
Yeah. And Ridley.

00:59:40.140 --> 00:59:42.560
Ripley. I call her, I call it Ripley.

00:59:42.800 --> 00:59:44.880
Oh, I call it Ridley because Ridley Scott.

00:59:44.880 --> 00:59:45.960
It is Ridley Scott.

00:59:46.120 --> 00:59:49.780
Okay, yeah. I thought, like, the Ripley thing was like, well, close, but no cigar.

00:59:49.900 --> 00:59:53.240
And then one day I was like, wait a minute, Ridley Scott directed a movie.

00:59:54.520 --> 01:00:00.420
Ripley is what I used to troll Mike with when we would talk about this game, and I've just

01:00:00.420 --> 01:00:02.740
accidentally made it my lexicon.

01:00:02.900 --> 01:00:05.380
I love it. It's like Binding of Isaac.

01:00:05.680 --> 01:00:06.600
Binding of Isaac.

01:00:06.820 --> 01:00:08.320
Isaac, yeah. Or Maria.

01:00:08.320 --> 01:00:10.540
I forgot which way I say it because now I know the correct way.

01:00:10.540 --> 01:00:11.500
But it is Isaac, right?

01:00:11.680 --> 01:00:14.940
It's Isaac. I'm actually hearing you guys talk about it so often, I actually don't know.

01:00:15.500 --> 01:00:16.880
We need to get back on track.

01:00:16.900 --> 01:00:19.040
So let's talk about how development started, okay?

01:00:19.800 --> 01:00:24.200
When making a game, we'd start out by having all the developers gather together to contribute

01:00:24.200 --> 01:00:27.040
their ideas with Gunpei Yokoi at the head of the group.

01:00:27.220 --> 01:00:31.680
Then, using everything we came up with as a base, we work on filling in the blanks.

01:00:31.680 --> 01:00:37.320
Because that's Sakamoto talking, but that would not be the case for this game because instead

01:00:37.320 --> 01:00:47.400
they gave this game to two brand new employees, Kiyotake and I think it was Hirofumi Matsuka.

01:00:47.800 --> 01:00:49.320
Those two were given the game.

01:00:49.320 --> 01:00:53.580
The only thing they had worked on before this was Game & Watch, okay?

01:00:53.740 --> 01:00:55.180
Whoa. The LCD games.

01:00:55.340 --> 01:00:59.780
So now it's like, hey, just go ahead and can you just make a major video game for us?

01:00:59.780 --> 01:01:03.160
Just you two. We'll join in later, but just go to town on it.

01:01:03.660 --> 01:01:04.980
Okay. So they did.

01:01:05.200 --> 01:01:07.100
And it's like, that's pretty inspiring, right?

01:01:07.100 --> 01:01:10.040
Yeah. Like these two dudes just wound up like making Metroid, right?

01:01:10.500 --> 01:01:11.640
Wrong. Okay.

01:01:12.520 --> 01:01:16.620
How long did it take for the two of you new employees to develop the game?

01:01:17.060 --> 01:01:19.480
Kiyotake says, not quite 10 months.

01:01:20.180 --> 01:01:22.320
Okay. Which is a weird answer to begin with, okay?

01:01:22.420 --> 01:01:23.860
Not quite 10 months.

01:01:24.020 --> 01:01:25.100
So nine months and 20 days.

01:01:25.100 --> 01:01:26.340
Nine months and 30 days, yeah.

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:27.880
That's 10 months.

01:01:28.060 --> 01:01:29.100
Well, 31 days, right?

01:01:29.100 --> 01:01:31.820
I always think of the longest months when I think of months.

01:01:32.160 --> 01:01:37.760
Okay. Sakamoto says, I didn't join development of Metroid until about the last three months.

01:01:37.760 --> 01:01:38.580
And he laughs.

01:01:39.060 --> 01:01:41.500
Sakamoto then adds, I wasn't the only one to join.

01:01:41.760 --> 01:01:43.960
Everyone in R&D 1 joined at the end.

01:01:44.080 --> 01:01:46.480
To be honest, it was hardly done at all.

01:01:48.700 --> 01:01:54.580
He jokes that like by the time that the rest of R&D 1 had joined, those two had done nothing

01:01:54.580 --> 01:01:56.500
in the first 10 months of it.

01:01:57.200 --> 01:01:58.280
That's really messed up.

01:01:58.680 --> 01:02:02.420
Figured out like, oh, we have to make a video game and try to like, imagine like just trying

01:02:02.420 --> 01:02:04.120
to learn. Like imagine you don't know.

01:02:04.200 --> 01:02:07.240
Right. C, you know, C plus or C sharp, right?

01:02:07.320 --> 01:02:08.900
What is it? What's the programming language?

01:02:10.580 --> 01:02:12.100
You mean like, imagine you're me.

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:16.520
Yeah. Imagine you're me, not knowing what the programming language that you're supposed to learn is.

01:02:16.740 --> 01:02:19.540
I do appreciate how blunt these interviews are.

01:02:19.660 --> 01:02:23.520
Like, not like the interviews now where it's like, oh, it was just such a great experience.

01:02:23.660 --> 01:02:25.560
Even if it's just for like NAC 2.

01:02:25.900 --> 01:02:28.440
Right. Like it wasn't a great experience.

01:02:28.460 --> 01:02:31.460
Are you kidding? They wrote the games in assembly, by the way.

01:02:31.540 --> 01:02:32.380
I do know that.

01:02:32.600 --> 01:02:33.560
Okay. Anyway.

01:02:33.960 --> 01:02:35.980
So Sakamoto says that it was hardly done at all.

01:02:35.980 --> 01:02:40.760
Well, everywhere had the same backgrounds and you could only do the same things.

01:02:40.920 --> 01:02:44.620
The characters were moving, but the rest of the game design was mere bare bones.

01:02:45.020 --> 01:02:48.840
So then they talk about how Sakamoto got into video games in general.

01:02:48.980 --> 01:02:50.320
And I'm like, okay, I'm on board for that.

01:02:50.400 --> 01:02:53.120
Like, how does one get into video games in the early 1980s?

01:02:53.180 --> 01:02:56.180
Because you got to remember Sakamoto was there in like 84.

01:02:56.180 --> 01:02:58.460
I think he joined into the maybe 83, 84.

01:02:58.760 --> 01:03:04.280
He says, one day, however, Shigeru Miyamoto wandered over to our room and asked, is anyone

01:03:04.280 --> 01:03:05.780
here willing to lend us a hand?

01:03:05.980 --> 01:03:08.680
That's how I ended up doing sprite work on Donkey Kong Jr.

01:03:09.580 --> 01:03:14.760
So you can see that like those two working on that video game is not a rare situation

01:03:14.760 --> 01:03:20.440
at all. You know, it's like maybe now they're like half presidents of Nintendo at this point.

01:03:20.460 --> 01:03:24.660
You know, it's like you just get assigned to something, you wing it, and then somehow

01:03:24.660 --> 01:03:25.820
like you succeed.

01:03:27.360 --> 01:03:31.700
So he became the, he did the sprite art for Donkey Kong Jr., which turned out to be my

01:03:31.700 --> 01:03:33.720
first real experience with video games.

01:03:34.000 --> 01:03:36.880
After that, I worked on the arcade version of Wrecking Crew.

01:03:37.280 --> 01:03:40.480
At the time, game development was done on a very small scale.

01:03:40.480 --> 01:03:45.320
So we had a team of five or six people working on everything from the programming to the music.

01:03:45.540 --> 01:03:47.920
Our development period was about half a year.

01:03:48.160 --> 01:03:49.320
That sounds surprising.

01:03:49.660 --> 01:03:51.460
Like from what we've heard so far.

01:03:51.680 --> 01:03:55.300
Yeah. Six months to make a game sounds about right for, for what they're asking for.

01:03:55.520 --> 01:04:00.560
You know, you hear so often now, I mean, we, you know, Telltale Games just had that whole

01:04:00.560 --> 01:04:05.560
like breakdown where they're no longer a company and they had to, now they're pretty much bankrupt

01:04:05.560 --> 01:04:07.860
that to sell their assets and they're being sued.

01:04:08.460 --> 01:04:12.960
But it's like, the story there is like, there's so many employees, there's so many deadlines,

01:04:13.260 --> 01:04:17.140
there's so many games to make, and they take so long to make, you know?

01:04:17.160 --> 01:04:21.820
It just turns into some like, uh, ah shit, nevermind.

01:04:21.980 --> 01:04:26.080
What if like, just to continue that thought, like what if the Legend of Zelda, like the

01:04:26.080 --> 01:04:30.240
original Legend of Zelda team got like the people, you know, the people who made Breath

01:04:30.240 --> 01:04:33.600
of the Wild, like transport to the past and go talk to them and be like, oh, you guys

01:04:33.600 --> 01:04:34.960
are making a Zelda game? We're making one too.

01:04:34.960 --> 01:04:36.760
We, we've been working on it for seven years.

01:04:37.280 --> 01:04:41.760
You know, like that's, that was the development cycle for Breath of the Wild was seven years.

01:04:42.040 --> 01:04:45.140
And it's like the Legend of Zelda team probably got like six months.

01:04:45.980 --> 01:04:47.120
Okay. It's not that long.

01:04:47.580 --> 01:04:48.560
What? Six months.

01:04:48.700 --> 01:04:50.220
Six months? No, I don't think it's that long at all.

01:04:50.320 --> 01:04:53.200
No. And I appreciate that they could make a game like this in that kind of time.

01:04:53.360 --> 01:04:56.840
Yeah. So, uh, on Samus's name.

01:04:57.060 --> 01:04:58.780
Okay. Everybody knows Samus Aran.

01:04:59.440 --> 01:05:04.820
Uh, Kiyotake was the one to name Samus, but it comes from a mispronunciation of the football

01:05:04.820 --> 01:05:06.660
legend Pele. What?

01:05:07.120 --> 01:05:09.580
Yeah. Right? That's exactly what I'm talking about.

01:05:09.700 --> 01:05:11.740
Right? How could there be a mispronunciation?

01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:12.820
Right? Oh my God.

01:05:12.920 --> 01:05:17.580
Like that drastic. So, apparently, Pele thought that, uh, Pele.

01:05:18.960 --> 01:05:22.160
Kiyotake. Kiyotake is now in this game development cycle, yeah.

01:05:22.160 --> 01:05:29.020
Kiyotake thought that Pele's real name was Samus Aranadas Nashimantels or something like

01:05:29.020 --> 01:05:29.880
How do you re-wet?

01:05:30.180 --> 01:05:32.000
Fucking with him. Name was super long.

01:05:32.180 --> 01:05:40.720
Name was Pele. And, um, and if you look it up, it's actually Edson, uh, Edson Arantes.

01:05:40.720 --> 01:05:47.260
So, he, he thought that it was Samus Arantes and named it Samus Aran, but Pele's real name

01:05:47.260 --> 01:05:48.340
is Edson Arantes.

01:05:48.900 --> 01:05:51.960
So, imagine if they just named it Pele.

01:05:52.240 --> 01:05:53.520
Yeah. Like, we just played as Pele.

01:05:53.620 --> 01:05:54.980
Pele Aran. Man.

01:05:55.220 --> 01:05:58.320
Um. Well, why name, why, why was Pele?

01:05:58.620 --> 01:05:59.900
Well, he just liked Pele.

01:06:00.060 --> 01:06:00.980
The inspiration. Yeah.

01:06:01.120 --> 01:06:04.820
That's cool. Yeah. You would think, like, where's the, where's the alien inspiration, right?

01:06:04.920 --> 01:06:06.260
Where's the Ridley Scott inspiration?

01:06:06.260 --> 01:06:07.500
Yeah. We also like soccer.

01:06:07.500 --> 01:06:09.100
Yeah, so, yeah, they like soccer.

01:06:09.100 --> 01:06:12.260
It does not take much for Nintendo to name a character after you.

01:06:12.260 --> 01:06:14.140
That's true. I mean, Mario is based after their landlord.

01:06:14.140 --> 01:06:19.300
Well, that's like in Metroid Prime 4, I hear that a character leaked, that's, uh, Cristiano

01:06:19.300 --> 01:06:22.020
Ronaldo. He's coming.

01:06:22.660 --> 01:06:25.080
Uh, by now, Smash is out, probably, right?

01:06:25.480 --> 01:06:28.320
Yeah. So, I hope you're enjoying it, future us.

01:06:28.880 --> 01:06:32.320
Uh, next, okay, so obviously, if we're gonna talk about Samus' name, we have to talk

01:06:32.320 --> 01:06:34.060
about how they decided to make Samus a woman.

01:06:34.880 --> 01:06:39.420
The instruction manual, of course, states that Samus is shrouded in mystery, but the

01:06:39.420 --> 01:06:43.420
team decided fairly early on whether it was gonna be a man or a woman, Sakamoto says.

01:06:43.640 --> 01:06:48.320
Once we entered the final stages of development, we started talking about having different endings

01:06:48.320 --> 01:06:50.860
depending on how long it took players to clear the game.

01:06:51.020 --> 01:06:52.960
Now, you can remember that kind of from Kid Icarus.

01:06:53.100 --> 01:06:56.180
Mm-hmm. There's different endings based on, uh, how well you...

01:06:56.760 --> 01:06:58.800
Or poorly. Oh, yeah, how well or poorly you did.

01:06:58.800 --> 01:07:03.440
This time, they decided to decide how well or poorly you do by how fast you can clear

01:07:03.440 --> 01:07:07.520
the game. So, they wanted a reward for people who cleared it more quickly.

01:07:07.900 --> 01:07:09.240
Kiyotake then adds,

01:07:09.380 --> 01:07:13.520
We wondered what would surprise everyone, and we talked about removing Samus' helmet.

01:07:14.240 --> 01:07:16.340
Then someone said, this is Sakamoto,

01:07:16.700 --> 01:07:20.720
It would be a shocker if Samus turned out to be a woman, and everyone thought that would

01:07:20.720 --> 01:07:23.460
be interesting and wanted to do it, so we decided it right away.

01:07:23.980 --> 01:07:24.980
Kiyotake then adds,

01:07:24.980 --> 01:07:27.320
Yeah, we decided that in a flash.

01:07:27.640 --> 01:07:31.840
Back then, people played games over and over, so we wanted to give a reward for playing through

01:07:31.840 --> 01:07:36.640
quickly. Then we decided to put it in four endings, with Samus removing her helmet, her suit,

01:07:36.720 --> 01:07:37.520
and so forth.

01:07:39.280 --> 01:07:41.220
Jeez. Yeah. Good thing it ended in five endings.

01:07:41.420 --> 01:07:44.560
And, yeah. And, you know, it's interesting.

01:07:44.740 --> 01:07:49.460
It's like, I think about it, and it's like, maybe it could have been, like, an Athena situation.

01:07:49.720 --> 01:07:52.220
Remember, we're talking about, like, the red bikini that's all she's wearing.

01:07:52.780 --> 01:07:57.480
But it's like, at the same time, it's like, I think this is a powerful moment for, you

01:07:57.480 --> 01:07:58.940
know, like, the female gamer, right?

01:07:59.080 --> 01:08:02.920
Like, I don't think it's like, it's like, powerful until you get to the last one, but

01:08:02.920 --> 01:08:06.560
like, under 30 minutes, and it's like, just her in her underwear or bikini or something.

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:09.500
Well, yeah, it's her in her bikini, and she's got, like, that green hair.

01:08:10.200 --> 01:08:13.200
Not on purpose, by the way, Samus doesn't actually have green hair, it's just that that's

01:08:13.200 --> 01:08:15.120
how they had to design it.

01:08:15.160 --> 01:08:16.280
With the palette. Yeah, with the palette.

01:08:17.280 --> 01:08:21.360
But, but I don't know, I'm not saying, I'm not saying that, like, you go girl, okay?

01:08:21.620 --> 01:08:25.200
I'm not saying it's that kind of empowerment, but I'm saying, like, it's definitely not,

01:08:25.360 --> 01:08:28.900
like, trying to be, like, look at this naked woman who you control.

01:08:29.240 --> 01:08:34.400
Right. I think there's more positives in this game than there are in Athena, but there's still

01:08:34.400 --> 01:08:35.460
definitely negatives. Right.

01:08:35.520 --> 01:08:41.500
Yeah. I think, like, having the helmet off and revealing that it's a woman, that would probably be the

01:08:41.500 --> 01:08:47.220
best scenario, you know, like, to end it there, but, like, you know, it became a big thing

01:08:47.220 --> 01:08:51.880
of, like, that time, like, oh, put in the Justin Bailey coat and play her, you know, without

01:08:51.880 --> 01:08:53.680
her clothes, you know, without her suit on.

01:08:53.860 --> 01:08:56.620
